{"acceptedClaimRefs":[{"assessmentDigest":"sha256:93ebc48965953337c8c70b303ffdf4bfff09cc0f966e0ac0da25539928c78e44","claimKey":"bssc-sum-capacity/two-letter-output-covariance-curvature","judgmentId":"sha256:141c4c2658e3662b874c8df5bd25eedffebed7155677cc2181a64c9bddda36af","transactionId":"906894532b971ecf8c4ac6fcdd3f7380e00549b2"}],"acceptedClaims":[{"claimKey":"bssc-sum-capacity/two-letter-output-covariance-curvature","dependencyTransactionIds":["9bb22afe5abd3e1d9f419c1717bd61bb33a958ff"],"statement":"For every input law on the governed half-skew BSSC product channel P^{otimes 2}, let c_in=P(X_1X_2=11)-Pr[X_1=1]Pr[X_2=1] and C_out=I(Y_1;Y_2)+I(Z_1;Z_2). A paired-receiver binary-table curvature argument proves the unconditional inequality C_out >= 31 c_in^2/(20 ln 2). Canonical transaction 9bb22afe5abd3e1d9f419c1717bd61bb33a958ff supplies the necessary inequality C_out < 7/160 for any finite two-letter Marton law whose unnormalized value exceeds twice the optimal one-letter randomized-time-division value. Consequently every such gain forces |c_in| < sqrt(7 ln 2/248) < 7/50. This is a necessary condition only; it does not construct a Marton gain, rule one out, tensorize Marton's functional, or give a capacity converse."}],"evidenceChunkDigests":["sha256:48cafb76a9fb8682784d85548ea8abb532ef28c4f496605a83227887b2b85998","sha256:60838ff19a5328d411bb4aa9f7c8bc78a2445d26bdf41a0740e2d08b2ad4a7bc","sha256:9764fcc6d169eaa82f04d483f6dec405c8df5eff3f9c94bcee3dd46a1ee40b69","sha256:d76ac238ad4a54b7c6cbe365f63cba591210de2924186bb05d18314c17dbb33c"],"evidenceManifest":{"chunkSizeBytes":65536,"contributionPath":"problems/bssc-sum-capacity/contributions/two-letter-output-covariance-curvature","files":[{"bytes":7402,"chunks":[{"bytes":7402,"digest":"sha256:60838ff19a5328d411bb4aa9f7c8bc78a2445d26bdf41a0740e2d08b2ad4a7bc","offset":0,"ordinal":0}],"digest":"sha256:60838ff19a5328d411bb4aa9f7c8bc78a2445d26bdf41a0740e2d08b2ad4a7bc","path":"problems/bssc-sum-capacity/contributions/two-let